AB  - Traditional hydrological models struggle to simulate streamflow in karst regions with seasonal rivers due to limited karst structure representation and dry-wet event recognition. Based on the SWAT framework, this study developed a karst SWAT model (KSWAT) and proposed an innovative wet-dry-event-karst SWAT model (WDE-KSWAT) by incorporating a feedback mechanism using the normalized improved weighted average precipitation index (NIWAPI). Performance tests at two hydrological stations in the Diaojiang karst river basin show that the WDE-KSWAT achieves optimal results (R2, NSE, KGE >0.75; PBIAS < ±20 %), significantly improving simulation accuracy and reducing bias in the SWAT and KSWAT across flow magnitudes. The WDE-KSWAT reveals strong seasonal variability in karst hydrology, with intensive matrix-conduit exchange in areas of dense underground rivers. The conduit rapid flow, epikarst spring flow, and matrix slow flow contribute approximately 75.2 %, 15.1 %, and 9.7 % to total spring discharge, respectively. This study provides a novel modeling strategy for streamflow simulation in karst regions.
