AB  - In the context of accelerated urbanization and extreme weather, predicting urban waterlogging depth is vital for disaster prevention. Existing models combining recurrent neural networks and graph neural networks rely heavily on long-term historical data, limiting their ability to capture current features. This paper proposes a waterlogging prediction model (TGC) integrating Transformer, GRU, and ChebNet, using waterlogging depth and precipitation data. TGC enhances spatial connections via an adjacency matrix based on distance, employs the Transformer's self-attention for parallel sequence processing, and combines ChebNet for spatial features with GRU for temporal trends. Experiments show TGC improves current-moment feature capture, achieving an RMSE of 2.13 cm, MAE of 0.86 cm, and MAPE of 5.09 %, demonstrating strong predictive performance for urban waterlogging decision-making.
