AB  - Fire is a major disturbance factor in grassland and forest ecosystems. Accurate assessments of grassland and forest fire risk and analyses of their drivers are crucial for improving existing fire prevention and control patterns. In this study, multi-criteria decision analysis methods were used to construct a fire risk assessment model for grasslands and forests; the spatiotemporal dynamics of fire risk from 2000 to 2021 were analyzed; and the dominant drivers affecting fire risk changes in different vegetation types were explored. The results indicated that: COPRAS is the optimal model for simulating grassland and forest fire risk. From 2000 to 2021, the area of grasslands and forests with increased fire risk (65.07 %) was significantly greater than that with decreased fire risk (34.93 %). In the four vegetation types, there were differences in the dominant drivers of fire risk. The dominant drivers showed significant spatial heterogeneity in the influence on fire occurrence.
