AB  - This study contributes a generalizable framework for evaluating water availability and drought vulnerability in institutionally complex river basins. The Delaware River Basin (DRB) is used to demonstrate the framework by stress testing its modern operations using a historical drought storyline based on a statistical streamflow reconstruction of the 1960s drought of record. An ensemble of reconstructed streamflows (1945–2023) is generated using results from the National Hydrologic Model, a probabilistic flow duration curve-based prediction in ungauged basins methodology, and Bayesian bias correction. Reconstructed streamflows are input to Pywr-DRB to simulate water management operations. Results reveal that New York City (NYC) reservoirs would be strongly depleted under the 1960s drought with modern management. Drought of this severity reveals significant vulnerabilities to NYC’s drinking water supply and tensions with controlling saltwater intrusion risks to Philadelphia’s water supply. The reconstruction and storyline drought stress testing framework contributed here has broad applicability to other river basin systems.
