AB  - This study extends an established two-dimensional flow measurement approach to three-dimensional scenarios, addressing the growing need for accurate and efficient non-contact measurement techniques in complex hydrodynamic environments. Compared to conventional Acoustic Doppler Current Profilers (ADCPs) and remote sensing-based flow monitoring, the proposed method enables high-resolution, continuous water velocity measurement, making it well-suited for hazardous environments such as floods, strong currents, and sediment-laden rivers. Building upon the original approach, we develop an enhanced model that incorporates multiple emission directions and flexible configurations of receivers. These advancements improve the adaptability and accuracy of the method when applied to three-dimensional flow fields. To evaluate its feasibility, extensive numerical simulations are conducted to mimic real-world hydrodynamic conditions. The results demonstrate that the proposed method effectively handles diverse and complex flow field configurations, highlighting its potential for practical applications in water resource management and hydraulic engineering.
