AB  - Surrogate-assisted optimization for computationally expensive models is often hampered by the prohibitive cost of sampling, especially in high-dimensional spaces. Conventional uniform sampling is inefficient because optimization success relies more on local fidelity near the optimum than on broad global accuracy. To address this, we introduce the SHAP-Guided Two-stage Sampling (SGTS-LHS) method, which integrates model interpretability into the experimental design. The method begins with a sparse global exploration using Latin Hypercube Sampling (LHS). A preliminary surrogate is trained and analyzed with SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) to rank global parameter importance. This ranking defines a high-potential region of interest for a second, denser sampling stage to locally refine the surrogate. We validated SGTS-LHS on three test cases. For a 2D multimodal function, it consistently guided the optimizer to the global optimum. For a high-dimensional analytical function, it correctly identified the sparse subset of critical parameters. Applied to a 15-dimensions groundwater transport model inversion, SGTS-LHS yielded a more accurate and robust hydraulic conductivity field than conventional sampling under an identical computational budget. By strategically reallocating computational resources, SGTS-LHS builds a more reliable local landscape to better guide optimization, offering a pragmatic solution for resource-constrained scientific and engineering problems.
